      "id": "90d7cc6b-901b-47cb-9d03-4ceeb239031e",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "2. Complementary CMOS: NOT, NAND, NOR, AND, OR",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "9d44fdc2-8bdb-48fd-94d1-419844fdccd1",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "a6bfc777-d638-48bb-b149-ce6a364b6bf3",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "027213d4-9e60-40bb-a615-71c09655e9d1",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "62f7e58b-8de8-41e4-b180-a9c84b872ee1",
      "text": "Static complementary CMOS replaces the resistor with a **PMOS pull-up network (PUN)**. It pairs that with an **NMOS pull-down network (PDN)**.\n\n- Derive when the output must be 0: that is the PDN conduction condition $\\overline F$.\n- AND in a conduction condition means switches in series. OR means parallel branches.\n- Build the PUN by swapping series and parallel and replacing NMOS with PMOS, keeping the same gate signals.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "d94655e2-35e3-417c-ba15-38f4b63a714b",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "2cb9376d-f262-4bec-9464-c8c2cc297f6b",
      "text": "```digital\n{\"kind\":\"cmos\",\"title\":\"CMOS inverter\",\"pdn\":{\"gate\":\"A\"}}\n```\n\n*A=0 turns the PMOS on and NMOS off; A=1 does the reverse.*",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "d94655e2-35e3-417c-ba15-38f4b63a714b",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "c9a385d3-c361-449c-9e74-c589120c6f15",
      "text": "For a valid steady binary input, the ideal complementary network provides one path to a supply rail while the other network is off. Real CMOS still has leakage and dissipates energy during switching; \u201czero static power\u201d is an ideal approximation.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "Two NMOS + two PMOS = four transistors.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "160a1b46-e3c6-46a1-a6a8-6ba60b570e4b",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "c37515d6-cadc-4ae7-a461-099b75195611",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "489fd5c1-a38c-4c4f-b830-80f9faa5451e",
      "prompt": "Design static CMOS for $F=\\overline{AB}$.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "The PDN must conduct only when both inputs are high.",
          "latex": "\\overline F=AB"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Put NMOS A and B in series. The dual PUN has PMOS A and B in parallel.",
          "latex": ""
        },
        {
          "explanation": "At A=B=1 the PDN is on. Otherwise at least one PMOS provides a pull-up path.",
          "latex": ""
        }
      ],
      "title": "NAND: trace both networks",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "160a1b46-e3c6-46a1-a6a8-6ba60b570e4b",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "c0eb61fc-92ea-41d9-aa96-ab1cf2081565",
      "text": "```digital\n{\"kind\":\"cmos\",\"title\":\"CMOS NAND\",\"pdn\":{\"series\":[{\"gate\":\"A\"},{\"gate\":\"B\"}]}}\n```\n\n*PMOS gate bubbles indicate conduction for a low gate input.*",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "NOR uses four transistors with the opposite series/parallel arrangement. Basic AND uses six.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "0f75d82b-a650-4aff-bb6b-dd14d7037e3c",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "ee06f3fd-67e5-440c-8f7f-bdf2a78ab43d",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "c53c734d-3033-471b-b981-76f16b7be86c",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "2c807d25-8114-4f77-83ef-abec768bd1b6",
      "prompt": "How does a 2-input NOR differ from a 2-input NAND? How many transistors implement a 2-input AND in basic static CMOS?",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "NOR needs parallel NMOS and series PMOS.",
          "latex": "F=\\overline{A+B}"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "AND is a NAND followed by an inverter.",
          "latex": "N_{\\mathrm{AND}}=4+2=6"
        }
      ],
      "title": "NOR and transistor counting",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "0f75d82b-a650-4aff-bb6b-dd14d7037e3c",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "ea1ee86c-d149-4af2-b325-d256462cdd48",
      "text": "```digital\n{\"kind\":\"cmos\",\"title\":\"CMOS NOR\",\"pdn\":{\"parallel\":[{\"gate\":\"A\"},{\"gate\":\"B\"}]}}\n```\n\n*Parallel NMOS pull down when either input is 1. Series PMOS pull up only for A=B=0.*",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "c53c734d-3033-471b-b981-76f16b7be86c",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "7b8e339b-023f-4277-b9b8-28ad7344661a",
      "text": "For basic complementary static CMOS, an n-input NAND/NOR uses n NMOS + n PMOS. An AND/OR adds a two-transistor inverter after the corresponding NAND/NOR. Count the gates actually drawn before considering a cheaper equivalent implementation.",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "48 transistors. Replacing the drawing with a different equivalent circuit answers a different question.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "3f47b2f5-ed20-41e4-bfef-75ecc81968ac",
          "source_id": "8f4eb7d8-fdc9-4966-827e-880bca61bef1",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "28153684-5a6b-4560-b778-6dd5e018b5e4",
      "prompt": "The supplied gate drawing has three NOTs, four 3-input ANDs, and one 4-input OR. How many transistors implement those gates in basic static CMOS?",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "The NOTs each use 2 transistors.",
          "latex": "3(2)=6"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Each 3-input AND is a 3-input NAND followed by an inverter.",
          "latex": "4(2\\cdot3+2)=32"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "The 4-input OR is a 4-input NOR followed by an inverter.",
          "latex": "2\\cdot4+2=10"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Add the counts for this drawn realization.",
          "latex": "N=6+32+10=48"
        }
      ],
      "title": "PS2 Q1: count the circuit that was given",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "027213d4-9e60-40bb-a615-71c09655e9d1",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "1c4f629e-ec1e-4877-8402-15b84224a2ce",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "c0c1c7e6-a246-4b9e-922b-3e0f81374430",
      "level": 1,
      "text": "3. Function \u2192 pull-down \u2192 dual pull-up",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "answer": "Three NMOS + three PMOS = six transistors. The output is high when A=0, or when B=C=0.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "027213d4-9e60-40bb-a615-71c09655e9d1",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "3bcfe160-00f3-4ebc-b56d-5ec737c45444",
      "prompt": "Implement $F=\\overline A+\\overline B\\,\\overline C$.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Complement the function to obtain the PDN condition.",
          "latex": "\\overline F=A(B+C)"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Use NMOS A in series with parallel NMOS B and C.",
          "latex": ""
        },
        {
          "explanation": "The dual PUN is PMOS A in parallel with series PMOS B and C.",
          "latex": ""
        }
      ],
      "title": "Lecture complex gate: derive the conducting paths",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "027213d4-9e60-40bb-a615-71c09655e9d1",
          "source_id": "43433cc2-ac9b-4f48-9f8f-e352ed8b54e2",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "1323314f-d451-44d0-b6fd-6b1b3bf1d2ba",
      "text": "```digital\n{\"kind\":\"cmos\",\"title\":\"Complex complementary CMOS gate\",\"pdn\":{\"series\":[{\"gate\":\"A\"},{\"parallel\":[{\"gate\":\"B\"},{\"gate\":\"C\"}]}]}}\n```\n\n*Always distinguish the function at f from the condition that makes the PDN conduct.*",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "answer": "Six transistors total. This one complex gate gives the inverted function; an extra inverter would produce AB+C.",
      "citations": [],
      "difficulty": "intermediate",
      "id": "4889553c-5031-4481-ba18-79830572cda7",
      "origin": "original",
      "prompt": "Build the PDN and count transistors for $F=\\overline{AB+C}$.",
      "solution": "",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "The PDN condition is AB+C: a series A\u2013B branch in parallel with C.",
          "latex": ""
        },
        {
          "explanation": "The PUN is parallel PMOS A/B in series with PMOS C. Each network has three transistors.",
          "latex": ""
        }
      ],
      "title": "Try it: preserve the outer inversion",
      "type": "practice_problem"
    },
    {
      "answer": "Three NMOS and three PMOS. The dual network keeps the same gate labels; PMOS already conducts on 0.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "3f47b2f5-ed20-41e4-bfef-75ecc81968ac",
          "source_id": "8f4eb7d8-fdc9-4966-827e-880bca61bef1",
          "version": 1
        }
      ],
      "id": "8058d258-02ae-45b8-9c92-8b5919fee75f",
      "prompt": "The given PDN has NMOS B in parallel with the series path A\u2013C, where A=x1, B=x2, C=x3. Derive the PUN and F.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "explanation": "Write the condition for a complete ground path.",
          "latex": "G=B+AC"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "The output is the complement of the ground-path condition.",
          "latex": "F=\\overline{B+AC}"
        },
        {
          "explanation": "Dualize: PMOS B is in series with parallel PMOS A and C.",
          "latex": "F=\\overline B(\\overline A+\\overline C)"
        }
      ],
      "title": "PS2 Q3: complete the missing PMOS half",
      "type": "worked_example"
    },
    {
      "citations": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "3f47b2f5-ed20-41e4-bfef-75ecc81968ac",
          "source_id": "8f4eb7d8-fdc9-4966-827e-880bca61bef1",
